The cache answers in 5 ms only for the 90% of requests it handles. The other 10% never get a cache answer and still take 200 ms, and an average has to count them too.

Those misses alone add 20 ms to the average (10% × 200 ms), so it can't be below 20 ms however fast the cache is. Even a cache that answered in 0 ms would give you a 20 ms average.

You can check this yourself in any JavaScript console:

```javascript
// Build a list of 1,000 request times: 900 cache hits at 5 ms and 100 misses at 200 ms
const requestTimes = [];

for (let requestNumber = 0; requestNumber < 1000; requestNumber++) {
    // The first 900 requests are cache hits, the remaining 100 are misses
    if (requestNumber < 900) {
        requestTimes.push(5);
    } else {
        requestTimes.push(200);
    }
}

// Add up every request time so we can divide by the number of requests
let totalTime = 0;

for (const requestTime of requestTimes) {
    totalTime = totalTime + requestTime;
}

// Prints 24.5
console.log(totalTime / requestTimes.length);
```

What is true is that the typical request takes 5 ms: that's the median, and it's what 9 out of 10 users will see. The mean is 24.5 ms.
